### Test 1: Are fatal crashes more common on weekends than weekdays (per day)?

```{r}
# Chi-square test: weekend vs weekday counts vs expected 5/7, 2/7
weekend_counts <- accident_weekend |>
  count(weekend)

chisq.test(weekend_counts$n, p = c(5/7, 2/7))

```

A chi-squared test was used to examine whether fatal crashes were distributed across weekends and weekdays based on the number of days each has (5/7 weekdays, 2/7 weekends). The results showed a significant difference (x-squared =281.66, df=1, p\< 0.001), indicating that fatal crashes occur at a higher rate per day on weekends than on weekdays.

### Test 2: Does vehicle type distribution differ between weekend and weekday crashes?

```{r}
# Chi-square test: vehicle type distribution by weekend/weekday
vehicle_weekend_table <- vehicle_plot |>
  filter(vehicle_type %in% top_vehicle) |>
  count(weekend, vehicle_type) |>
  pivot_wider(names_from = vehicle_type, values_from = n, values_fill = 0) |>
  column_to_rownames("weekend") |>
  as.matrix()

chisq.test(vehicle_weekend_table)

```

A chi-square test looking between vehicle type and weekend/weekday status was significant at the 0.05 p-level, indicating that the distribution of vehicle types involved in fatal crashes differs between weekends and weekdays.

### Test 3: Logistic regression- Is hour of day associated with a fatal crash occuring on the weekend?

```{r}
# Crash-level dataset for logistic regression: weekend vs weekday
accident_model_data <- accident_weekend |>
  filter(HOUR >= 0, HOUR <= 23) |>
  mutate(
    weekend_binary = if_else(weekend == "Weekend", 1L, 0L)
  ) |>
  dplyr::select(
    weekend_binary,
    HOUR,
    RUR_URBNAME,
    LGT_CONDNAME
  )

# Logistic regression model: weekend vs weekday
model_weekend_hour <- glm(
  weekend_binary ~ factor(HOUR),
  data   = accident_model_data,
  family = binomial
)

summary(model_weekend_hour)

```

A binary logistic regression was used to determine whether the time of day was associated with a fatal crash occurring on a weekend rather than a weekday. Compared with crashes occurring at midnight (which was the reference point), fatal crashes occurring between 1-3 am had higher odds of occurring on weekends. The strongest of those times occurred at 2 am, when fatal crashes had approximately 1.45 times the odds of occurring on a weekend compared with midnight crashes. On the other hand, crashes occurring from the morning through the evening generally had significantly lower odds of occurring on weekends.
## Question 2 Summary 
Overall, our findings support the alternative hypothesis that fatal-crash frequency, timing, and vehicle characteristics differ between weekends and weekdays. Fatal crashes occurred at a significantly higher rate per day on weekends than on weekdays, and weekend crashes were more concentrated during late-night and early-morning hours. Sedans and pickup trucks were the most common vehicle types in both groups; however, the chi-square test indicated that the overall distribution of vehicle types differed significantly by weekend versus weekday status.

